  "query_preview": "..Relatively benign conditions are expected\nareawide today on the backside of an upper-level trough currently\ncentered over the Northern Plains. Enhanced west to northwest \nflow across portions of eastern Utah, however, will result in \ngusty winds developing across the San Rafael Swell and western \nUinta Basin particularly in areas favored by downsloping. This \nwill result in another period of critical fire weather conditions \nthis afternoon and evening for these locations. Otherwise,\nconditions remain dry with near to slightly above normal\ntemperatures areawide today. \n\nThe aforementioned west to northwest flow across the region today\nwill also continue to transport both near surface and vertically\nintegrated smoke from numerous fires across the Pacific Northwest.\nSpecifically, high resolution guidance is showing a notable surge\nthis evening into Wednesday morning across northern Utah as near-\nsurface smoke currently pooled across the Snake River Plain gets \npulled along the Wasatch Front as winds switch more northerly \novernight. Across central and southern Utah, the Widemouth 2 Fire\nwill continue to push smoke into southeast and south-central Utah\nthrough Wednesday. While concentrations will fluctuate through the\nperiod, smoke is expected to linger through at least Thursday\nmorning as fires around the region remain active under hot and dry\nconditions. As such, expect hazy skies to continue especially\nacross central and northern Utah and southwest Wyoming, in\naddition to deteriorated air quality.\n\nHigh pressure developing across the western US mid-to-late week\nwill result in a warming trend into the weekend, with temperatures\npeaking on Saturday around 10 degrees above average. Widespread\nMajor to locally Extreme HeatRisk emerges Friday and Saturday for\nvalley locations, indicating heightened concerns for heat-related\nillness. HeatRisk remains elevated into Sunday for northern and\ncentral Utah valleys as overnight lows remain warm due to \nincreasing cloud cover. \n\nWhile moisture availability remains limited this week, chances for\nafternoon showers and thunderstorms emerge as early as Thursday\ngiven increased instability from intense heating under the ridge.\nDaily chances will continue through the weekend, with best chances\nfor development across the southern mountains. Given dry low\nlevels, the main threat for storms late week will be dry lightning\nand gusty and erratic outflow winds. There is potential for\nincreasing PWATs from a modest monsoonal surge into southwest Utah as\nearly as Sunday, which would bring an increasing risk for \nisolated flash flooding into early next week. However, confidence \nis low at this time on the coverage and magnitude of flash\nflooding potential.",
